A||||18||Dumba$$||fiero store possibly overpriced??||||8||
Z||000000||Dumba$$||04-27-2001||12:02 AM||||First off i am not putting down the fiero store  they have alot of hard to get things that alot of people need <BR>but,  here is what 2 instances of them bieng overpriced <BR>1, i needed polly. cradle mount bushings, <BR>fiero store price $69 i belive,<BR>so i did a little lookin and got them from NOPI for $34 <BR>now thats a damn big differance<BR>2, the 1/4 window arrowheads <BR>fiero store $9 ? i think<BR>dealership in Sioux Falls SD (where i live),<BR>2.50 each<P>those are just to instances where ive found a better deal  butt the polly bushings, <BR>thats a huge diff.<P>OK im done just had to blow off a little steam!!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE||||reg||8
Z||000001||Oreif||04-27-2001||12:15 AM||||Fiero Store is more expensive on items. But they do have some item's that are hard to get and some of their kit prices are not bad. Also you can get a 10% discount if you belong to a Fiero Club. (Discount does not apply for the genuine GM parts) As for the crests, you'd better check with the dealer first. Many parts are fading fast and they cannot order more. They only have whatever stock is left. Some dealers won't even try to get them. Also the F.S. is both of them for $9 and they give you a small tube of glue for them.<BR>Customer service and speedy delivery is a big asset too. Some place's charge more for shipping and it takes longer to get items. Where the F.S. has in cases FEDEX'd overnight to assist someone with a sick car.<P><BR>I bought my entire suspension package from them. With the 10% discount, I was able to save almost $100 than if I shopped around. The biggest part was the shipping. With the Fiero Store I got everything in one shipment, The shop around method would have cost me a lot more in shipping and would have taken longer to get everything. As an example you can buy Addco sway bars for about $99 + shipping But you need to order the sway bar poly kit with hardware for $39.95 + shipping. The Fiero Store sway bars are $149 each - 10% so it's $135.10 + shipping and they came with all the hardware and instructions. The entire suspension kit with all I bought was $880.00 shipped. If I priced it out from other sources it would have been $950 shipped. It is less expensive to shipp all from one place than many from many.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||operator64||04-27-2001||12:25 AM||||   The Fiero Store is great, i ordered some parts on monday the 23rd and had them the 25th i was not expexcting them for a week or so but they shipped them out promptly. Great service and i thought the prices ( on what i bought ) were fair.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Kelvin Vivian||04-27-2001||12:35 AM||||It definitely pays to shop around...<P>The Fiero Store sells a headlamp upgrade kit for $69.xx (gold, white, or blue tint)<P>At the time I was looking to buy, I checked eBay for a similar headlamp upgrade kit, and the prices started at $75.00. (I use eBay as a source to get a general "price check" on items whenever I can)<P>So I decided to buy a couple of kits from the Fiero Store. I sent payment over the net, and received the 2 kits promptly and was very happy with the service. I have yet to install the kits, I'm in DC, cars are in CA, and kind of can't wait to see what they look like, both are "white" tint.<P>But now, you can buy a similar upgrade kit from the "Dutch_high_bidders" on eBay starting at $50.00  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=582906716&r=0&t=0" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=582906716&r=0&t=0</A> <P>I have bought T-top locks from the "Dutch_high_bidders" on eBay before so if I were in the market now to buy a headlamp kit now, I might buy from these guys... But then again I might pay extra and go with the products sold from the Fiero Store since chances are greater that their products will fit/work in our Fieros without any problems.<P>Anywhoo, it does pay to be a $mart $hopper.<P>The Fiero Store headlamp kits will be going into these two babies in less than 2 weeks...<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1601741&a=12208700&p=44402874&Sequence=1&res=high">  <BR>88 GT, 5-spd<BR>87 SE 4-cyl, 5-spd||||reg||3
Z||000004||thomas_l||04-27-2001||09:23 AM||||Sometimes it's a matter of convenience. For example, I buy a lot of items at my local NAPA regional distribution center about 10 miles away knowing I could probably get the same things for less if I did some shopping. Most of the time, I want my stuff NOW so I pay extra for the convenience. There is hardly ever an occaision that they don't have an item stocked as they supply most NAPA stores within 200 miles. I would expect the same with Fiero Store. I bought new door locks and a rear hatch lock from them because the prices were OK and I didn't want to waste time shopping for a better deal. I ordered via the website and three days later the items were delivered and exactly what I wanted. There might have been "some guy" on eBay or other Internet site, but for 3 locks that cost just over $50 with shipping, Fiero Store was the ticket! I will probably go through them for my 4-speed shifter cables just due to the confidence that I will likely get the correct parts with minimum hassle. The guys at NAPA have trouble spelling Fiero much less getting creative on finding parts. The GM dealer? Fuggetaboutit. I might try Tom Jumper Chevy just cause they stock a LOT of inventory, but I'm not holding my breath on Fiero shift cables  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000005||Rodrv6||04-27-2001||11:04 AM||||Thomas, you might want to try Moore Pontiac up in Canton.  They have been VERY helpful to me in the past.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000006||fyerstarter||04-27-2001||11:41 AM||||All my experiences from the Fiero Store have been awesome. I order parts online which is a very big plus for me since I work from about 10am to 11pm, the parts are shipped very quickly. In fact right now I was getting a little worried because the floormats I ordered hadn't showed up in a week, I wrote Fiero Store and email last night and when I woke up this morning there was a reply waiting for me explaining they were shipped from the factory and should be on their way.<P>So far I give the Fiero Store 10/10.||||reg||1
Z||000007||firstfiero||04-27-2001||12:28 PM||||    Are their prices a little high...yep. Are their parts and shipping excellent...You betcha. I don't mind paying for service and quality!<P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif"> <BR>Todd <BR>  86se||||reg||14
Z||000008||stsmithgt||04-27-2001||02:33 PM||||I agree with thomas about convenience.  I don't want to waste my time to save a couple of dollars.  My time is worth more than saving 5 or 10 dollars by driving around for an hour to have to order it anyway and then have to go back to pick it up where I found it.  I ordered some things from the Fiero Shop and they were sitting on my front porch in four days.  That was just the way I like it.  I come home from a hard days work and have presents sitting there to unwrap. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||04-27-2001||04:06 PM||||I love the Fiero Store.  Some of their items may be a bit pricey, but not all.  They have great customer service, and have quickly corrected any problems I have had, which are very few.  I ordered a Fiero decklid sticker which got crinkled in shipping... they just sent a new one out to me, and I got the wrong part once which they immediately replaced.  <P>I also like to give them business since they are giving so much support to the Fiero.  As Pontiac becomes more useless for parts (I find the dealer useless when I buy a new car!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) it will be very nice to have a good resource for parts!  They are so much better than FOCOA its not even comparable.  I don't mean to put FOCOA down, but the Fiero Store is just much quicker and easier to deal with.<P>Doesn't Rodney Dickman sell the arrowheads too?   Although I haven't done any business with him yet, I see nothing but praise from his customers also!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Greg Piet||04-27-2001||05:56 PM||||I am of the opinion that if there is a company that I like and would like to see it in the future I will send as much business there as possible.  The Fiero Store gets most  of my business even though I have at times found cheaper prices elsewhere.  As of late I do not really go price hunting, as the Fiero Store is typically close in price, and I know they have what I want in stock 99% of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||04-27-2001||07:56 PM||||I shop everything.<P>Most places.... rear brake rotors.... 40-60$ each and must wait 1-5 days to get them.<P>a certain local parts store.... &lt;$30 each and got them delivered to store the same day, on a saterday even. (Have to go in early, other wise it's next day they are open.)<P>Many of Fiero Stores items are available from parts shops that cary ACdelco or from local dealers. They have very little that I haven't seen else where.<P>I've beat their prices repeatedly and that includes shipping on the stuff I mail ordered.<P>If you need the conveneince then they do have everything most people need in one place. They do have some of the "fluf" items for Fiero, keyrings, shirts, etc, you can't get anyplace else.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||04-27-2001||08:04 PM||||With the Fiero Store also you know your not gonna get ripped.  That alone is worth the extra dollars.  However sometimes they go a little overboard with prices but they have to make money somewhere because you won't make millions selling Fiero anything.  Sometimes I try to support them even if its a little cheaper somewhere else.  I mean, who would you rather support, NAPA or some other big chain, or the Fiero Store?  Consider the extra money as a donation to the Fiero cause hehe.<P><BR>my 2 cents||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||04-27-2001||08:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Greg Piet:<BR><B>I am of the opinion that if there is a company that I like and would like to see it in the future I will send as much business there as possible.  The Fiero Store gets most  of my business even though I have at times found cheaper prices elsewhere.  As of late I do not really go price hunting, as the Fiero Store is typically close in price, and I know they have what I want in stock 99% of the time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My Daddie used to tell me (& anyone else who would listen) that when you get a supplier who does what he's supposed to do WHEN he's supposed to do it, you stay with him. I always buy all my poly bushings & sway bars from them. A lot of the GM parts, I buy from GM, but for Suspension bushings, sway bars, Konis and that kind of stuff, I'll just get them from the Fiero Store. I buy a lot of these items from the Fiero Store & I'm sure I could shop around a little bit and save a few $$. However, Good, Honest, dependable service is worth a little more to me. That's why people that work on Fieros all the time buy from places like the Fiero Store and Rodney Dickman.<P>BTW, has anyone ever noticed that when you buy something from someone on EBay, the seller always gets a touch of the Flu before he ships your stuff. This always happens right after he's deposited your money and before he ships your goods.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000014||Dumba$$||04-27-2001||09:00 PM||||i agree with you guyes the fiero store is fast and have good service!!i have also ordered alot from them and never had a problem, butt if i can save $30<BR>on cradle mount bushings and wait 2 more days to me its worth the wait!!<BR> also, fiero store price for horn paddels $38<BR>dealership here in town $19 <BR>once again no shipping charges and to me its worth the 2-3 more days <P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||04-27-2001||10:28 PM||||10 years down the road when dealership has no more Fiero specific parts, then who are you going to turn to?  Ooops Fiero Store isn't around anymore, now what?  Support them while they are still around...<P>||||reg||12
Z||000016||Spektyr||04-27-2001||10:29 PM||||Hmm... in the last week or so I've ordered 5 things from the Fiero Store and Rodney Dickman combined (can't remember which one was 2 and which one was 3 things).  Everything made it in a timely manner and I was very pleased with the service I received.  It turns out that I can't really use the headlight motor rebuild kits I ordered since I don't want to drill out the rivets (not sure how to put it back together if I do), but that's my own fault since Rodney has the directions posted on his website and I didn't read them closely enough.  But a guy in town here (that I met through this forum) says his uncle has a pair of NOS motors he bought years ago in his attic.  I'm hoping those haven't been sold or tossed as that'd be the quick and easy way to fix the problem, and I'm sure he'll sell them cheaper than a retail outlet.<P>So where do I stand on the pricing issue?  I gladly pay premium prices for convienence and the knowledge that I'm going to get the parts I want.  And I like to support businesses that are important to me.  Imagine trying to keep a Fiero running with only the local parts stores and dealerships to rely on.  THAT would be a pain.<P>Hell, the local parts stores know me by sight and I've only had my Fiero a week.<P>"What the hell do you want now?" is the normal greeting I get.  They know I'm going to ask for something they don't have, probably can't order, and haven't ever been asked for before.  One guy didn't even know what my fuel evap canister was when I showed it to him (and those look like every other evap canister in use).<P>So Fiero-part shopping for me starts on the net, and usually ends up in the parts store looking for the thing I just broke fixing something else.  (BTW, don't ever put your foot on top of the fuel evap canister while you're wrestling with the even numbered spark plugs.)<P>I see posts from time to time bad-mouthing this or that parts store.  Yeah, things get screwed up sometimes.  Find a business that can honestly claim 100% customer satisfaction and zero customer relations problems.  I'll show you a place that went out of business before anyone could make a normal, human mistake.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Steve Normington||04-29-2001||01:23 AM||||[Fiero Store Props]<P>What I like best is the fact that I know the stuff I'm ordering will fit on a Fiero. With every one else, it's by guess and by golly if the stuff will fit or not.||||reg||1
Z||000018||maryjane||04-30-2001||03:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dumba$$:<BR><B>First off i am not putting down the fiero store  they have alot of hard to get things that alot of people need <BR>but,  here is what 2 instances of them bieng overpriced <BR>1, i needed polly. cradle mount bushings, <BR>fiero store price $69 i belive,<BR>so i did a little lookin and got them from NOPI for $34 <BR>now thats a damn big differance<BR>2, the 1/4 window arrowheads <BR>fiero store $9 ? i think<BR>dealership in Sioux Falls SD (where i live),<BR>2.50 each<P>those are just to instances where ive found a better deal  butt the polly bushings, <BR>thats a huge diff.<P>OK im done just had to blow off a little steam!!<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Aint you heard? ALL Fiero parts are worth their weight in gold! It's just a matter of comin up with the gold.<BR>||||reg||6
